885
00:37:12.370 --> 00:37:12.970
It does it for you.

886
00:37:13.030 --> 00:37:13.670
Well, there you go.

887
00:37:14.050 --> 00:37:19.410
Well, I got very interested in Joe's comment about the inventor of liquid paper.

888
00:37:19.930 --> 00:37:21.030
Did you deep dive?

889
00:37:21.350 --> 00:37:22.410
Yes, I did, Angela.

890
00:37:22.670 --> 00:37:24.630
And this story is amazing.

891
00:37:25.330 --> 00:37:28.910
Liquid paper was invented by Bette Nesmith Graham.

892
00:37:30.090 --> 00:37:37.990
In 1954, she was a divorced single mom supporting herself and her son as a secretary working at a bank in Texas.

893
00:37:37.990 --> 00:37:40.230
She made about $300 a month.

894
00:37:40.630 --> 00:37:41.550
Here's the thing, though.

895
00:37:41.910 --> 00:37:44.050
She was a really bad typiSt.

896
00:37:44.170 --> 00:37:49.850
And every time she had to correct her mistakes, you either had to kind of, like, erase it and then it would smudge.

897
00:37:49.850 --> 00:37:52.470
Or you'd just have to start all over again.

898
00:37:52.970 --> 00:37:55.350
Well, she was also a painter.

899
00:37:55.670 --> 00:38:02.830
And she thought to herself, you know, when painters make a mistake, they don't erase it or they don't start over.

900
00:38:03.050 --> 00:38:04.450
They just paint over it.

901
00:38:04.690 --> 00:38:14.870
So she was like, what if I took some of my white tempura paint to work and I painted over my mistakes and I let it dry and then I typed over it?

902
00:38:15.350 --> 00:38:16.230
Guess what?

903
00:38:16.430 --> 00:38:17.750
It freaking worked.

904
00:38:18.750 --> 00:38:23.610
But she was like, I need to make a better formula than just this straight paint.

905
00:38:23.830 --> 00:38:29.650
So in her kitchen at home with her blender, she created liquid paper.

906
00:38:29.850 --> 00:38:33.090
She put it in little nail polish bottles.

907
00:38:33.450 --> 00:38:33.970
Brilliant.

908
00:38:34.450 --> 00:38:38.750
And for five years, she and her coworkers at the bank used it.

909
00:38:38.830 --> 00:38:40.010
They called it mistake out.

910
00:38:40.950 --> 00:38:44.470
Well, then she got the idea, I should do something with this.

911
00:38:44.590 --> 00:38:45.270
Yes, you should.

912
00:38:45.390 --> 00:38:46.230
I'm going to make this a business.

913
00:38:46.230 --> 00:38:51.590
So she hired some teenagers for a dollar an hour to package it up in her garage.

914
00:38:51.590 --> 00:38:54.270
And they started shipping it out and sending it to places.

915
00:38:54.610 --> 00:38:56.170
She applied for a patent.

916
00:38:56.390 --> 00:38:57.510
It caught on.

917
00:38:57.670 --> 00:38:59.430
She renamed it liquid paper.

918
00:38:59.430 --> 00:39:04.090
And they started opening up factories in Dallas, Toronto, and Brussels.

919
00:39:04.510 --> 00:39:10.570
At its peak, they were making 25 million bottles of liquid paper every year.

920
00:39:10.870 --> 00:39:13.830
With her wealth, she established two foundations.

921
00:39:14.150 --> 00:39:20.490
One that gave grants and financial support to promote women in the arts and the other which supported women in business.

922
00:39:21.270 --> 00:39:26.370
But at one point, while her liquid paper business is booming, she decided to get married again.

923
00:39:26.810 --> 00:39:28.590
But guess what this guy did?

924
00:39:28.970 --> 00:39:31.490
Please don't tell me he took her money or sold her patent.

925
00:39:31.710 --> 00:39:32.610
Please don't tell me.

926
00:39:32.730 --> 00:39:37.770
He tried to push her out of her own company and take it over for himself.

927
00:39:38.350 --> 00:39:39.730
What a S-O-B.

928
00:39:39.730 --> 00:39:40.750
That's right.

929
00:39:41.090 --> 00:39:46.270
He even tried to change the formula of liquid paper ever so slightly so that—

930
00:39:46.270 --> 00:39:47.290
So he could patent it himself.

931
00:39:47.470 --> 00:39:49.870
So that he wouldn't have to pay her royalties on it.

932
00:39:50.150 --> 00:39:51.170
Well, guess what?

933
00:39:51.330 --> 00:39:52.450
She prevailed.

934
00:39:52.610 --> 00:39:57.230
She sold the company in 1979 for $47.5 million.

935
00:39:57.310 --> 00:39:59.950
She gave millions of dollars to charity.

936
00:40:00.550 --> 00:40:05.270
Sadly, she died six months later from a stroke after selling her company.

937
00:40:06.270 --> 00:40:12.470
And incidentally, her son is Michael Nesmith, the guitarist for The Monkees.

938
00:40:12.750 --> 00:40:14.390
The rock band The Monkees.

939
00:40:14.470 --> 00:40:15.150
It's his mom.

940
00:40:15.650 --> 00:40:17.290
Oh, my gosh.

941
00:40:17.470 --> 00:40:17.770
Right?

942
00:40:18.110 --> 00:40:20.110
That is a fascinating story.

943
00:40:20.390 --> 00:40:21.930
I loved that story.

944
00:40:22.070 --> 00:40:22.470
Me too.

945
00:40:22.770 --> 00:40:26.550
It's just a person solving a problem in their everyday life, you know?

946
00:40:26.810 --> 00:40:28.170
That's like my friend.

947
00:40:28.410 --> 00:40:29.690
Oh, the cupcake lady.

948
00:40:29.910 --> 00:40:30.410
Yes.

949
00:40:30.710 --> 00:40:32.310
My friend, Jennifer Gunn.

950
00:40:32.530 --> 00:40:33.910
She was a stay-at-home mom.

951
00:40:33.910 --> 00:40:37.770
She's constantly making cupcakes for her kids to take to school.

952
00:40:37.770 --> 00:40:40.470
And she was annoyed that they were getting all messed up.

953
00:40:40.690 --> 00:40:43.450
She's like, I can't keep putting them in like a shirt box.

954
00:40:43.570 --> 00:40:45.030
What am I transporting them in?

955
00:40:45.510 --> 00:40:47.850
So she came up with the cupcake courier.

956
00:40:48.190 --> 00:40:49.490
And she patented it.

957
00:40:49.590 --> 00:40:52.250
And she sold it out of her garage for years.

958
00:40:52.430 --> 00:40:54.570
And then finally, she sold the design.

959
00:40:54.570 --> 00:40:56.730
And it's in like Target and all these places.

960
00:40:56.870 --> 00:40:57.290
Yeah, you get it everywhere.

961
00:40:57.510 --> 00:40:58.410
Bed, Bath, and Beyond.

962
00:40:59.270 --> 00:40:59.670
Amazing.

963
00:41:00.010 --> 00:41:02.490
When she told me, I was like, how has no one thought of this before?

964
00:41:02.490 --> 00:41:06.710
It was this amazing, like, stackable cupcake transportation device.

965
00:41:06.910 --> 00:41:07.710
Josh has one.

966
00:41:07.750 --> 00:41:08.350
I know.

967
00:41:08.630 --> 00:41:10.130
I brought it on Rachel Ray.

968
00:41:10.370 --> 00:41:11.430
I was so excited about it.

969
00:41:11.490 --> 00:41:12.110
I was like, give me one.

970
00:41:12.230 --> 00:41:14.630
I'm giving one to Rachel Ray when I go on her talk show.

971
00:41:15.490 --> 00:41:16.310
Cupcake courier.

972
00:41:16.510 --> 00:41:17.170
Liquid paper.

973
00:41:17.710 --> 00:41:18.070
Mm-hmm.

974
00:41:18.250 --> 00:41:18.590
Mm-hmm.

975
00:41:19.490 --> 00:41:21.110
Women solving everyday probleMs.

976
00:41:21.390 --> 00:41:21.510
Yeah.

977
00:41:22.070 --> 00:41:28.690
During this town hall meeting, I have to point out how great they build this irritation that

978
00:41:28.690 --> 00:41:30.030
Joe has towards Michael.

979
00:41:30.290 --> 00:41:33.210
Well, he keeps, like, doing all these, like, sidebars.

980
