00:43:27.570 --> 00:43:29.450
Well, lady, I think we should take a break.

1028
00:43:29.610 --> 00:43:32.230
I do, too, because when we come back, the cat's in the cradle.

1029
00:43:32.610 --> 00:43:32.990
Mm-hmm.

1030
00:43:33.510 --> 00:43:34.390
Jim is feeling guilty.

1031
00:43:34.590 --> 00:43:35.710
Dwight's plan is working.

1032
00:43:36.350 --> 00:43:37.150
It's working.

1033
00:44:03.560 --> 00:44:08.180
This is the song that Dwight is playing while Jim is on a sales call.

1034
00:44:08.800 --> 00:44:09.200
Yeah.

1035
00:44:09.860 --> 00:44:11.400
I can't listen to that song.

1036
00:44:11.400 --> 00:44:13.020
It makes me too sad.

1037
00:44:13.180 --> 00:44:13.960
I'm so sorry.

1038
00:44:13.960 --> 00:44:15.520
I just had Sam play it.

1039
00:44:15.840 --> 00:44:17.540
I can't listen to the whole thing.

1040
00:44:17.740 --> 00:44:19.120
It bums me out too much.

1041
00:44:20.080 --> 00:44:24.240
Cats in the Cradle is a 1974 folk rock song by Harry Chapin.

1042
00:44:24.540 --> 00:44:26.160
I did not know this.

1043
00:44:26.420 --> 00:44:35.040
The song began as a poem written by his wife, Sandy, inspired by her ex-husband's awkward relationship with his father.

1044
00:44:36.420 --> 00:44:40.860
Chapin said the song is also about his own relationship with his son, Josh.

1045
00:44:41.400 --> 00:44:41.860
Mm-hmm.

1046
00:44:42.440 --> 00:44:50.200
Basically, I think you guys know what the song is about, but the reason Dwight's playing it is because it's about a man who becomes a father,

1047
00:44:50.200 --> 00:44:55.920
but he's just too busy to spend time with his son, and his son's always asking him, spend time with me.

1048
00:44:55.920 --> 00:44:58.320
And the dad's like, we'll find a good time later.

1049
00:44:58.520 --> 00:44:58.720
Yeah.

1050
00:44:58.900 --> 00:45:05.100
But then in the final verse, the son is all grown up, and the dad finally wants to spend time with him, but now the son is too busy.

1051
00:45:05.480 --> 00:45:11.580
And he realizes his son has grown up to be just like him, just too busy for relationship and intimacy.

1052
00:45:11.580 --> 00:45:15.600
This song makes me sad, and so does the book, The Giving Tree.

1053
00:45:15.840 --> 00:45:16.740
Always makes me sad.

1054
00:45:17.000 --> 00:45:17.980
Oh, right?

1055
00:45:18.260 --> 00:45:18.480
Ugh.

1056
00:45:19.840 --> 00:45:28.680
Randy told me that we had to pay for both the sync and the master rates to this song because we both hear the song, and then Dwight and Andy sing it,

1057
00:45:28.740 --> 00:45:32.280
and the total cost for this was $46,000.

1058
00:45:32.860 --> 00:45:42.860
Well, Jim is going to have a talking head, like you said, outside of Vance Refrigeration, and he's going to say he's not surprised that Dwight is using his baby to steal his desk,

1059
00:45:42.920 --> 00:45:45.440
but what he is surprised by is that it's working.

1060
00:45:45.920 --> 00:45:51.940
This next scene, Erin is going to bring Joe a fax, but she's so clearly sick.

1061
00:45:52.320 --> 00:45:58.820
Joe's reaction to Erin walking in the room sick, she's like, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, get out.

1062
00:45:59.420 --> 00:46:01.400
It made me laugh really hard.

1063
00:46:01.400 --> 00:46:04.060
Lady, am I, seriously, am I Joe Bennett?

1064
00:46:04.060 --> 00:46:05.100
I think you might be.

1065
00:46:05.220 --> 00:46:08.720
That is 100% how I react to a sick person walking into a room.

1066
00:46:08.820 --> 00:46:10.820
I'm like, oh, wait, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no.

1067
00:46:11.080 --> 00:46:12.560
Hi, not today.

1068
00:46:12.960 --> 00:46:15.300
I am not getting sick today because of you.

1069
00:46:15.540 --> 00:46:16.800
You need to go home.

1070
00:46:17.360 --> 00:46:19.320
I was like that before the pandemic.

1071
00:46:19.580 --> 00:46:21.600
I know, and now you're really leaning into it.

1072
00:46:21.640 --> 00:46:22.460
I really am.

1073
00:46:22.660 --> 00:46:24.260
Like, sniffle, goodbye.

1074
00:46:24.420 --> 00:46:25.080
Don't think so.

1075
00:46:25.860 --> 00:46:29.800
Well, Andy is super bummed out because Joe sends Erin home,

1076
00:46:30.640 --> 00:46:32.900
and now what are they going to do about their date?

1077
00:46:33.760 --> 00:46:35.160
Well, Andy has an idea.

1078
00:46:36.040 --> 00:46:37.500
He's going to go in to Joe.

1079
00:46:37.760 --> 00:46:41.380
He's going to have a bunch of coughing and a bunch of sickie.

1080
00:46:42.140 --> 00:46:43.460
Joe's going to send him home.

1081
00:46:44.660 --> 00:46:46.560
Everyone should have started hacking and coughing.

1082
00:46:46.800 --> 00:46:48.200
They all would have gotten to go to the bar.

1083
00:46:48.400 --> 00:46:49.360
You're so right.

1084
00:46:50.120 --> 00:46:53.140
Angela, have you ever pretended to be sick to get out of something?

1085
00:46:53.420 --> 00:46:55.140
Oh, my gosh, you're putting me on the spot.

1086
00:46:55.140 --> 00:46:56.180
I'm sure I have.

1087
00:46:56.780 --> 00:46:57.480
You know what?

1088
00:46:57.600 --> 00:47:00.840
One of the things that just comes to mind, which is so lame, Jenna,

1089
00:47:00.920 --> 00:47:03.740
but I'm really bad at lying.

1090
00:47:03.960 --> 00:47:04.700
I'm really bad.

1091
00:47:05.420 --> 00:47:07.040
And my dad was the same way.

1092
00:47:07.220 --> 00:47:09.080
Like, a police officer might pull him over for speeding,

1093
00:47:09.080 --> 00:47:10.780
and he'd be like, yep, I was.

1094
00:47:10.780 --> 00:47:11.360
You're correct.

1095
00:47:11.540 --> 00:47:12.280
You know what I mean?

1096
00:47:12.360 --> 00:47:12.540
Yeah.

1097
00:47:12.900 --> 00:47:15.280
One time I was working at 1-800 DentiSt.

1098
00:47:15.460 --> 00:47:17.820
You know, I was in my 20s, and I had this 6 a.m.

1099
00:47:17.960 --> 00:47:18.280
shift,

1100
00:47:18.720 --> 00:47:19.880
and I overslept.

1101
00:47:20.300 --> 00:47:23.560
And my manager called me, and he was so nice.

1102
00:47:24.020 --> 00:47:25.080
I answered the phone.

1103
00:47:25.240 --> 00:47:25.780
Pretty groggy.

1104
00:47:25.900 --> 00:47:26.420
I was like, hello.

1105
00:47:27.300 --> 00:47:30.080
And he said, oh, Angela, are you not feeling well?

1106
00:47:30.300 --> 00:47:31.740
I said, no, I'm fine.

1107
00:47:33.020 --> 00:47:33.840
And he said—

1108
00:47:33.840 --> 00:47:35.560
You had such a chance to get out of it.

1109
00:47:35.620 --> 00:47:37.880
He said, if you're not feeling well, I can write you up.

1110
00:47:37.940 --> 00:47:38.260
It's sick.

1111
00:47:38.340 --> 00:47:38.780
It's fine.

1112
00:47:39.000 --> 00:47:40.140
I was like, no, no, no.

1113
00:47:40.220 --> 00:47:40.960
I'm just tired.

1114
00:47:42.200 --> 00:47:45.960
He was like, okay, well, can you wake up then and come to work?

1115
00:47:46.040 --> 00:47:47.600
I said, yeah, yeah, I'll be there.

1116
00:47:49.660 --> 00:47:53.740
And I told my roommates that, and they were like, he literally gave you an out.

1117
00:47:53.860 --> 00:47:54.160
Twice.

1118
00:47:54.360 --> 00:47:54.980
I know.

1119
00:47:55.240 --> 00:47:56.380
And I was like, no, no.

1120
00:47:57.960 --> 00:48:01.840
Well, I have a crazy story about pretending to be sick, and it involves Molly Shannon.

1121
00:48:02.100 --> 00:48:02.260
What?

1122
00:48:02.260 --> 00:48:05.400
Speaking of Molly Shannon, who's come up twice now on this podcaSt.

1123
00:48:05.420 --> 00:48:05.960
What happened?

1124
00:48:06.400 --> 00:48:06.960
All right.

1125
00:48:07.140 --> 00:48:11.840
So when I first came to L.A., I was working as a typist for the Television Critics Association.

1126
00:48:12.780 --> 00:48:16.840
All the stars of all of the new TV shows gather.

1127
00:48:17.100 --> 00:48:18.720
We've gone to them for The Office.

1128
00:48:18.720 --> 00:48:25.680
And you do a panel, and you talk to the Television Critics Association about your new show, about what you're doing.

1129
00:48:25.900 --> 00:48:26.120
Yeah.

1130
00:48:26.300 --> 00:48:33.500
And then in the evenings, they would have these cocktail parties where the cast of these shows would mingle with the press and give quotes.

1131
00:48:33.720 --> 00:48:33.900
Right.

1132
00:48:34.160 --> 00:48:36.760
Well, when I first moved here, I was a typiSt.

1133
00:48:36.760 --> 00:48:43.840
And what I did was I would attend these panels, and I would make notes, and then I would get an audio tape,

1134
00:48:43.880 --> 00:48:47.700
and I would go into a little hotel room that was all decked out with computers.

1135
00:48:47.700 --> 00:48:54.060
And there was a whole group of us shoved in this little hotel room with computers, and I would have to transcribe them.

1136
00:48:54.060 --> 00:49:01.840
And you would have to transcribe them by the end of the day, and then we would give these transcriptions to all the members of the Critics Association.

1137
00:49:02.520 --> 00:49:02.920
Wow.

1138
00:49:03.240 --> 00:49:07.180
So the first time we ever did this press, you knew exactly how it worked.

1139
00:49:07.220 --> 00:49:07.820
I did.

1140
00:49:07.960 --> 00:49:08.320
Oh, my gosh.

1141
00:49:08.320 --> 00:49:19.400
And I actually told that story on the stage when I was up on the stage, and I sent a big basket of cookies to all the typists who I knew were typing everything we were saying.

1142
00:49:19.600 --> 00:49:20.520
That's so great.

1143
00:49:20.800 --> 00:49:27.980
So one of the things about this job that was super cool was that if we got our work done on time, we got to go to the parties.

1144
00:49:28.280 --> 00:49:29.960
Oh, at the end of the day.

1145
00:49:29.960 --> 00:49:35.160
Yes, and just be like a fly on the wall and looky-loo at all the celebs.

1146
00:49:35.160 --> 00:49:35.210
Yes.

1147
00:49:35.340 --> 00:49:44.840
So this one night, I heard that the cast of Saturday Night Live was going to be at the party, and I wanted to go so badly.

1148
00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:46.560
But their party was early.

1149
00:49:46.560 --> 00:49:48.620
It was the 5 o'clock party.

1150
00:49:48.620 --> 00:49:50.040
We never got done by 5 o'clock.

1151
00:49:50.680 --> 00:49:53.140
But I was like, I have to go to that party.

1152
00:49:53.360 --> 00:49:54.140
I have to.

1153
00:49:55.520 --> 00:50:00.860
So like two days before, I started pretending like I was a little bit sick.

1154
00:50:01.540 --> 00:50:03.180
And then on the day of the party-

1155
00:50:03.180 --> 00:50:04.560
You were the slow play.

1156
00:50:04.560 --> 00:50:05.140
I was.

1157
00:50:05.300 --> 00:50:07.860
I was like, oh my God, I'm just like not getting better.

1158
00:50:08.020 --> 00:50:08.940
I'm not getting better.

1159
00:50:09.360 --> 00:50:11.180
But I was also being paid by the hour.

1160
00:50:11.360 --> 00:50:13.380
So, you know, I had to, that was the thing.

1161
00:50:13.520 --> 00:50:13.980
Like I had to

1162
00:50:13.980 --> 00:50:17.680
gradually get sicker because I needed to work until 4 o'clock.

1163
00:50:18.160 --> 00:50:19.460
You know, I needed the money.

1164
00:50:20.300 --> 00:50:25.160
So the thing was, was like it was very clear to me that we weren't going to make it to this party.

1165
00:50:25.260 --> 00:50:26.740
We were not going to get our work done.

1166
00:50:26.960 --> 00:50:27.140
Right.

1167
00:50:27.140 --> 00:50:30.520
So I said to my boss, I think I gotta go.

1168
00:50:30.680 --> 00:50:31.320
I'm so sorry.

1169
00:50:31.440 --> 00:50:32.600
I'm not going to make it to the end of the

1170
00:50:32.600 --> 00:50:32.880
day.

1171
00:50:33.140 --> 00:50:35.420
And my boss was like, of course, just go, go.

1172
00:50:35.700 --> 00:50:36.980
Get good reSt. We'll see you tomorrow.

1173
00:50:37.080 --> 00:50:37.320
I'm like,

1174
00:50:37.400 --> 00:50:39.040
thank you so much.

1175
00:50:39.040 --> 00:50:40.120
So I left at 4 o'clock.

1176
00:50:40.300 --> 00:50:44.400
I went to my car where I had a bag with makeup and a

1177
00:50:44.400 --> 00:50:45.160
cocktail dress.

1178
00:50:45.580 --> 00:50:46.600
Holy crap, Jenna.

1179
00:50:46.880 --> 00:50:47.080
Yeah.

1180
00:50:47.240 --> 00:50:50.580
And I put it on and then I realized I couldn't get back in

1181
00:50:50.580 --> 00:50:53.320
because all I had was my work badge.

1182
00:50:53.740 --> 00:50:57.000
Well, that's when I noticed the red carpet.

1183
00:50:57.420 --> 00:50:59.140
Did you walk the red carpet?

1184
00:50:59.380 --> 00:51:00.140
Sort of.

1185
00:51:00.560 --> 00:51:00.780
What?

1186
00:51:01.160 --> 00:51:05.000
So back then there like wasn't a lot of security.

1187
00:51:05.300 --> 00:51:05.480
No.

1188
00:51:05.960 --> 00:51:11.160
After the limos would drop celebrities at the carpet, I noticed they didn't look for any

1189
00:51:11.160 --> 00:51:11.600
credentials.

1190
00:51:11.780 --> 00:51:14.380
If you got dropped off in a limo, you just got to walk in.

1191
00:51:14.760 --> 00:51:15.320
Hold up.

1192
00:51:15.440 --> 00:51:18.600
Were you like hiding in the bushes waiting for a limo to pull up and then you

1193
00:51:18.600 --> 00:51:20.280
would just scooch in behind the people?

1194
00:51:21.020 --> 00:51:21.380
No.

1195
00:51:21.380 --> 00:51:22.540
That's a good idea, though.

1196
00:51:22.660 --> 00:51:25.040
That's what mom detectives would do because you know they

1197
00:51:25.040 --> 00:51:26.240
love hiding in a good bush.

1198
00:51:26.240 --> 00:51:26.920
They love a bush.

1199
00:51:28.540 --> 00:51:28.900
So.

1200
00:51:29.280 --> 00:51:29.940
Oh, Lord.

1201
00:51:31.000 --> 00:51:32.960
So I saw the line of limos.

1202
00:51:33.120 --> 00:51:35.600
After they would drop people off, they would just go line up and wait

1203
00:51:35.600 --> 00:51:37.300
for people to be done like for hours.

1204
00:51:37.520 --> 00:51:38.780
They just wait till the end of the night.

1205
00:51:39.100 --> 00:51:40.180
So I went up and I

1206
00:51:40.180 --> 00:51:45.900
just tap-tapped on a window and I was like, hey, would you be willing to-

1207
00:51:45.900 --> 00:51:46.900
To drop me off?

1208
00:51:46.940 --> 00:51:49.980
Drop me off at the carpet?

1209
00:51:51.300 --> 00:51:53.880
I cannot believe this story.

1210
00:51:54.280 --> 00:51:57.560
You knocked on the window to a perfect stranger and said, hey,

1211
00:51:57.780 --> 00:52:01.720
will you make a loop through the cul-de-sac and give me a little ride to the front door?

1212
00:52:01.880 --> 00:52:02.140
Yeah.

1213
00:52:02.360 --> 00:52:04.300
I'm trying to fake you my way in.

1214
00:52:04.460 --> 00:52:04.720
Yeah.

1215
00:52:04.980 --> 00:52:06.780
And he was like, sure, why not?

1216
00:52:06.960 --> 00:52:08.020
Oh, my Lord.

1217
00:52:08.100 --> 00:52:08.860
He was like, no problem.

1218
00:52:08.860 --> 00:52:12.140
So he dropped me off at the red carpet.

1219
00:52:12.540 --> 00:52:15.320
I did not walk the front of the carpet.

1220
00:52:15.440 --> 00:52:16.280
I did not walk

1221
00:52:16.280 --> 00:52:17.420
the part where they take your picture.

1222
00:52:17.560 --> 00:52:20.300
I walked the back part of it, you know, where the publicist

1223
00:52:20.300 --> 00:52:20.540
walked?

1224
00:52:20.620 --> 00:52:21.000
Yeah, yeah.

1225
00:52:21.160 --> 00:52:21.380
Okay.

1226
00:52:21.540 --> 00:52:22.540
I walked in.

1227
00:52:22.720 --> 00:52:25.140
I went right into the SNL party.

1228
00:52:25.360 --> 00:52:26.660
It was amazing.

1229
00:52:26.840 --> 00:52:28.300
I couldn't believe it.

1230
00:52:28.660 --> 00:52:31.780
I spotted Molly Shannon from across the way.

1231
00:52:32.520 --> 00:52:35.160
And I was like, I have to say something to this woman.

1232
00:52:35.160 --> 00:52:36.780
I mean, she's everything to me.

1233
00:52:37.440 --> 00:52:39.820
So I went up.

1234
00:52:40.020 --> 00:52:42.060
She was talking with a group of press.

1235
00:52:42.180 --> 00:52:42.640
And when they

1236
00:52:42.640 --> 00:52:48.520
kind of walked away, I just said, Molly Shannon, I just got here to LA.

1237
00:52:48.640 --> 00:52:49.840
I haven't even been here a

1238
00:52:49.840 --> 00:52:50.320
year yet.

1239
00:52:50.420 --> 00:52:53.000
And I just want to say you're such an inspiration to me.

1240
00:52:53.080 --> 00:52:54.520
And I love you so much.

1241
00:52:54.900 --> 00:52:55.880
And she

1242
00:52:55.880 --> 00:52:57.480
looked at me, Angela.

1243
00:52:58.120 --> 00:53:01.000
She put her hands on my shoulders.

1244
00:53:01.200 --> 00:53:03.580
And she said, don't give up.

1245
00:53:04.200 --> 00:53:04.740
Don't

1246
00:53:04.740 --> 00:53:05.020
give up.

1247
00:53:05.100 --> 00:53:07.220
Whatever you do, you do not give up.

1248
00:53:07.300 --> 00:53:10.880
It took me 10 years to land on Saturday Night Live.

1249
00:53:11.220 --> 00:53:12.660
10 long years.

1250
00:53:12.880 --> 00:53:15.760
But once I did, my whole life changed.

1251
00:53:15.920 --> 00:53:17.740
So you just don't give up, okay?

1252
00:53:17.740 --> 00:53:18.560
Ah.

1253
00:53:19.620 --> 00:53:20.060
Ah.

1254
00:53:20.620 --> 00:53:23.960
Do you know what that means to a young artist?

1255
00:53:24.200 --> 00:53:24.600
Yeah.

1256
00:53:24.880 --> 00:53:26.580
New to town to hear that?

1257
00:53:27.060 --> 00:53:27.580
Well,

1258
00:53:27.760 --> 00:53:32.180
I went home that day and I told my mom this encounter.

1259
00:53:32.460 --> 00:53:35.360
And for years, for years, my mom

1260
00:53:35.360 --> 00:53:38.980
would say to me, Jenna, don't you forget what Molly Shannon said to you.

1261
00:53:39.120 --> 00:53:40.480
Don't you forget, honey.

1262
00:53:40.640 --> 00:53:42.100
Oh, that's going to make me cry.

1263
00:53:42.100 --> 00:53:42.480
I know.

1264
00:53:42.480 --> 00:53:43.480
I'm getting emotional.

1265
00:53:43.480 --> 00:53:44.860
Well, lady.

1266
00:53:44.860 --> 00:53:47.100
Your mom quotes Molly Shannon to you.

1267
00:53:47.160 --> 00:53:47.340
To me.

1268
00:53:47.520 --> 00:53:47.840
Oh.

1269
00:53:48.200 --> 00:53:49.180
Listen to this.

1270
00:53:49.380 --> 00:53:50.600
So it was eight years

1271
00:53:50.600 --> 00:53:52.660
until I landed on the office.

1272
00:53:53.620 --> 00:53:56.920
10 years later, my movie Walk Hard came out.

